baseSourceLocations.controllingText:
19.1 Byte authority

An agent-provided filename or hash is a claim.
The application-computed SHA-256 of actual stored bytes is authority.

A required attachment is accepted only when the application verifies:
- expected canonical artifact relationship;
- expected filename or authorized filename pattern;
- media type where contracted;
- actual byte size;
- SHA-256 calculated from actual bytes.

19.2 Custody honesty

Browser-local artifact custody does not imply that:
- an external AI has the bytes;
- an independent reviewer has the bytes;
- a repository runner has the bytes;
- CAD software has the bytes;
- a laboratory has the bytes;
- an external portal has the bytes.

The UI and prompt identify the exact transfer action.

The package is one closed-loop-handoff-container/1 logical container and contains only the current files and records required by the operation. Its manifest conforms to closed-loop-verification-package/1 and includes:

- packageId;
- operationReservationId;
- challengeNonce;
- contractProfileId;
- jobId;
- stage;
- operation;
- targetSlot;
- expectedRevision;
- current scope and scopeHash;
- prompt identity;
- container-contract identity;
- exact `instruction.txt` identity;
- every outbound member role, canonical path, filename, byte length, media type, hash algorithm, digest, disclosure classification, and availability;
- every withheld category and reason;
- every expected ATTACHMENT_SLOT_ID and return contract;
- required response filename and schema;
- external-action risk classification and authorization IDs;
- validity conditions.

The authoritative prompt and manifest are always exported. Additional context and product files are exported only when required. Blind-alias mappings remain in the application-controlled context manifest and are not exposed to a blind reviewer. The complete project is never exported merely to perform one operation.

An optional archive representation is accepted only under the registered deterministic archive profile and must contain exactly the manifest-declared entries. The logical package identity remains the canonical manifest digest and member digests, not picker order or archive entry order.

Returned files are selected into their named attachment slots. The application verifies actual bytes, membership, slot relationships, reservation, package, prompt identity, revision, scope, and response contract before proposal creation.

